## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of the Trie (prefix tree) data structure, covering insert, delete, and prefix-query operations with proper edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie node structure**: Each node stores 26 child pointers, an end-of-word flag, and a pass-through counter for efficient prefix queries
- **Safe deletion**: Decrement counts along the path and only unmark end-of-word when the word actually exists
- **Prefix query**: Return the count at the prefix's last node in O(length of prefix) time
- **Duplicate handling**: Adding the same word twice increments the counter twice; deleting decrements twice, effectively removing one occurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.isEndOfWord = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  add(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
      }
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count++;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = true;
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return;
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count--;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = false;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return 0;
      node = node.children[index];
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Models real build/dependency graphs where costs accumulate; asks for both memoized and tabular DP to compare patterns and understand trade-offs. Emphasizes modulo arithmetic and edge-case handling for unreachable sinks and tight budgets.

## Key Concepts

- DP on DAG with an extra weight dimension
- Top-down memoization with (node, remainingBudget)
- Bottom-up DP in topological order with weight axis
- Modulo arithmetic and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized DFS
function countPaths(n, edges, W) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) adj[u].push([v, w]);
  const memo = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(W + 1).fill(-1));
  const dfs = (u, rem) => {
    if (u === n - 1) return 1;
    if (memo[u][rem] !== -1) return memo[u][rem];
    let res = 0;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) if (w <= rem) res = (res + dfs(v, rem - w)) % 1_000_000_007;
    return memo[u][rem] = res;
  };
  return dfs(0, W);
}
```

```javascript
// Bottom-up DP in topological order
function countPathsBottomUp(n, edges, W) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }
  // Kahn's algorithm for topo order
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const topo = [];
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift(); topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(W + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= W - w; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] !== 0) dp[v][t + w] = (dp[v][t + w] + dp[u][t]) % 1_000_000_007;
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= W; t++) ans = (ans + dp[n - 1][t]) % 1_000_000_007;
  return ans;
}
```

